# Strigiformes

Strigiformes is the order that contains the owls, split between the barn owls of
[[tytonidae]] and the typical owls of [[strigidae]]. Its members share a broad
skull, large forward-facing eyes set in a disc of stiff feathers, and a
reversible outer toe that closes the foot around a struggling rodent. Almost
every species is active after dark, and the flight feathers carry a velvety pile
and a comb-like fringe that damp the sound of the wingbeat to nothing. The order
is cosmopolitan, absent only from Antarctica and the remoter oceanic islands.
Owls swallow prey whole and cast the indigestible fur and bone as a pellet,
which is why a roost can be read from the ground beneath it.
